%T A400202 20,7,17,11,13,11,26,9,14,13,24,9,24,9,21,19,16,9,32,12,22,16,24,11,
%U A400202 30,17,30,19,21,13,43,12,20,23,30,17,34,13,28,20,34,14,48,13,23,26,31,20,37,15,44
%N A400202 Number of integers k, 1 <= k < n, such that (n+k)/gcd(n,k) is prime.
%C A400202 Equivalently, a(n) is the sum, over the divisors d > 1 of n, of the number of primes strictly between d and 2d.
%C A400202 By Bertrand's postulate, each divisor d > 1 contributes at least one term, hence a(n) >= tau(n) - 1 for n > 1.
%C A400202 For prime n, a(n) = A060715(n).
%H A400202 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400202/b400202.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A400202 a(n) = Sum_{d|n, d>1} (pi(2*d - 1) - pi(d)).
%F A400202 a(n) = Sum_{d|n} A060715(d).
%F A400202 A060715(n) = Sum_{d|n} mu(n/d)*a(d).
%e A400202 For n = 6, the values (6+k)/gcd(6,k), for k = 1,...,5, are 7, 4, 3, 5, 11. Four of these values are prime, so a(6) = 4.
%e A400202 Equivalently, the divisors d > 1 of 6 are 2, 3, and 6. There is one prime strictly between 2 and 4, one between 3 and 6, and two between 6 and 12. Hence a(6) = 1 + 1 + 2 = 4.
%t A400202 A400202[n_] := DivisorSum[n, PrimePi[2*# - 1] - PrimePi[#] &];
%t A400202 Array[A400202, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 27 2026 *)
%o A400202 (Python)
%o A400202 from sympy import divisors, primepi
%o A400202 def a(n):
%o A400202     return sum(int(primepi(2*d - 1) - primepi(d)) for d in divisors(n) if d > 1)
%o A400202 (Python)
%o A400202 from math import gcd
%o A400202 from sympy import isprime
%o A400202 def a(n):
%o A400202     return sum(isprime((n+k)//gcd(n,k)) for k in range(1,n))
%Y A400202 Cf. A060715.
%K A400202 nonn,easy,new
%O A400202 1,4
%A A400202 _Tomas Laris_, Sep 21 2026
